"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.7. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pT2 stage, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. The absence of removed lymph nodes, noted as no lymph nodes were removed, widens uncertainty regarding nodal status, as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. Biopsy results showed a difference, with the first biopsy indicating Gleason 0+0, and the second showing Gleason 3+4 with perineural invasion.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of extraprostatic extension and no seminal vesicle invasion. The positive surgical margins represent a risk factor. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this prognosis. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of regional disease control. The fixed estimated horizon is 80.98 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 52.64–109.33 months; its coverage has not been validated."
